Still the reason that English is dominant in the world of science has a 
lot to do with America.  Before WW 2, there were actually scientific 
articles written in German, because Germany was leading in science 
(well, physics) at the time.  After WW 2, this lead went over to the US.
Now, of course the reason that the US speaks English is due to the fact 
that most of the power inside the country was concentrated on English 
descendants.  So it has a lot to do with power and nothing with an 
inherent superiority of the language which is probably absent (although 
I do like English a lot).

> Second, English is remarkably flexible.  One can speak it
> with a very bad accent or rearrange the word order and still
> be understood.  

Granted.

> But a slight change of inflection in 
> Chinese or a re-arrangement of word order in German, and
> and one draws only blank stares.  

I don't know about Chinese, but I do know about German.  There you are 
wrong.  Changing the word order in German is not a big deal.  People 
will still understand you.  In fact, English is less flexible in that 
respect (I learned at school that positional adverbs always have to come 
before temporal ones in English.  In German, it is usually the other way 
round, but you are free to make exceptions.)

> English is also powerful
> because it easily absorbs words from other languages.  

This seems ridiculous to me.  English *creates* new words, because new 
inventions are mostly produced in the US and have to be described in 
English for the international community.  *Other* languages *absorb* the 
English words to a much higher degree than vice versa.  English is 
rather sterile in that.  (This was of course different in the past, many 
Latin or French words have migrated into English.)

> English
> vocabulary is massive compared to every other language in
> the world.

It is larger than that of the other European languages (with the 
possible exception of French), however not because it absorbs from them 
but rather because *they* absorb from English.  This is more a 
*consequence* of English being dominant than a *reason* of its 
dominance.  Germans are pragmatic, they just absorb English words like 
"computer" into their language, to the extent that they speak "Denglish" 
  ("D" from "Deutsch"=German, "english" should be clear).  The French do 
that in everyday language, too, but in addition they have the "Academie" 
which translates English words, so instead of "computer" they have 
"ordinateur".  Since there is an official translation for most English 
words, French has a vocabulary that is comparable to the English one - 
although many of the official translations are not used.  Instead people 
use the English expressions...  As a consequence, the "active" French 
vocabulary is smaller than the English one.  But there is certainly not 
a "massive" or overwhelming superiority in the number of words on the 
side of English.

Concerning Chinese, I believe it has a larger vocabulary than English.
